-- Migration 18: EVL-04 datasets + the golden schema, and the empty `experiments`
-- grouping table item 9 (EVL-02) extends.
--
-- Six tables. Spec: `specs/EVL-04-datasets-and-the-golden-schema.md` section 2.2.
--
--
-- ── DO NOT EDIT THIS FILE AFTER IT HAS BEEN APPLIED ─────────────────────────
--
-- ClickHouse migrations in this repo are applied PER FILE BY HAND, statement by
-- statement, with no journal table recording which ran --
-- `scripts/apply-migration-03.sh:23-45` is the worked example and it hardcodes
-- one migration path. There is nothing that would notice a file changing after
-- it was applied, so an edited file is a schema that diverges from prod
-- silently, in the direction nobody checks. The postgres side of this repo has
-- already paid for the un-journaled shape once (, a HALF-APPLIED migration
-- 14 that nothing recorded -- see `scripts/ci/audit-migration-drift.py`).
--
-- So: `experiments` below is created EMPTY-ish and OWNED BY ITEM 9. Item 9 adds
-- what it needs with `ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN` (and, if it wants EVL-02's
-- column spelling, `ALTER TABLE ... RENAME COLUMN`, which is cheap on a table
-- with no rows) in a NEW migration `19_...`. Never by editing this file.
--
--
-- ── TWO TYPE TRAPS THIS REPO HAS ALREADY PAID FOR ───────────────────────────
--
-- 1. `Nullable(LowCardinality(String))` is ILLEGAL in ClickHouse 24.12
--    ("Nullable cannot wrap LowCardinality"). It is the reason migration 03
--    never applied to prod on its first attempt
--    (`infra/dev/clickhouse/migrations/03_prompt_promotion.sql:37-40`). Every
--    nullable text column here is a plain `Nullable(String)`.
-- 2. `ReplacingMergeTree(<nullable column>)` is likewise rejected -- migration
--    03 records it at `:64-67`, where `eval_runs` had to drop to the
--    version-less form because `completed_at` is `Nullable`. Every version
--    column used below (`updated_at`) is NOT NULL, so the versioned form is
--    legal here.
--
--
-- ── DateTime64(3) MEANS MILLIS, AND GETTING IT WRONG IS SILENT ──────────────
--
-- Every timestamp column here is `DateTime64(3, 'UTC')`. `clickhouse-rs` maps a
-- plain `i64` straight onto the column's RAW TICKS with no unit conversion, so
-- the precision in this DDL is the only thing that decides what an inserted
-- number means. A `timestamp_micros()` at an insert site does not error and does
-- not truncate. MEASURED on 24.12.6.70 through the RowBinary path the Rust client
-- actually uses: the same instant written as MICROS is ACCEPTED with no error and
-- reads back as `2299-12-31 23:40:00` -- DateTime64's ceiling, where the formatter
-- clamps a value that is arithmetically 1,755,859,200,000 seconds past the epoch.
-- No time-range query will ever find that row and nothing complains.
-- (`datetime64_millis_now`'s own doc comment in
-- `crates/gateway/src/clickhouse_query.rs` describes the same failure as landing
-- "in the year ~48000". The scaled arithmetic is indeed out there, but
-- everything that RENDERS the value says 2299. The lesson is identical.)
-- THIS SHIPPED TWICE on the sibling tables (`promotion_decisions.decided_at`,
-- then `rollback_events.fired_at`), which is why the writer is a shared function
-- rather than a convention: `datetime64_millis_now()` in
-- `crates/gateway/src/clickhouse_query.rs`. Use it at EVERY insert site below.
--
--
-- ── NO ROW POLICIES, DELIBERATELY ───────────────────────────────────────────
--
-- Migration 03 declares five and prod has `count() FROM system.row_policies = 0`
-- instance-wide -- none was ever applied, and they could not work anyway because
-- the gateway never sets the `X-Tenant-Id` header they test. Declaring one here
-- would add a control that has never functioned, which is worse than no control
-- because it reads as one (`CLAUDE.md` rule 1). Isolation is the explicit
-- `WHERE tenant_id = ?` bind in every query, proven by a two-tenant test and by
-- `scripts/ci/check-tenant-isolation.py`, which has NO exemption mechanism.
-- Every ORDER BY below therefore starts with `tenant_id`, because every read
-- filters on it and nothing else prunes.
--
--
-- ── PARTITIONING: ONE RULE, TWO REASONS ─────────────────────────────────────
--
-- Five of the six tables are `PARTITION BY tuple()` -- no partitioning:
--
--   (a) A partition key earns its keep by pruning reads. Every read on these
--       tables filters `(tenant_id, <id>)` and NEVER a time range, so a monthly
--       partition would prune nothing while multiplying the part count.
--   (b) On a ReplacingMergeTree a partition key derived from a column the update
--       path rewrites is the B-243 shape: ClickHouse merges parts ONLY WITHIN a
--       partition, so two versions of one row that land in different partitions
--       can never collapse. `datasets` / `dataset_items` / `experiments` are
--       read-modify-written on rename, tombstone and `expected_output`
--       write-back, and `toYYYYMM(created_at)` would silently depend on the
--       writer carrying the ORIGINAL `created_at` forward every single time.
--       Migration 15 removed exactly this key for exactly this reason
--       (`infra/dev/clickhouse/migrations/15_trace_summaries_aggregating.sql`,
--       and `trace_summaries` in `infra/dev/clickhouse/schema.sql:109-112`
--       carries a TTL with `PARTITION BY tuple()`, so a TTL does not require
--       partitions either).
--
-- `eval_run_items` is the ONE exception and keeps `PARTITION BY
-- toYYYYMM(started_at)`. It is the only table here that is UNBOUNDED -- every
-- run that has ever executed, ~200 rows each, forever -- it is append-only so
-- reason (b) cannot apply, `started_at` never changes, and if a retention
-- window is ever added it can drop a partition instead of mutating the table.
-- EVL-02 states month partitions for it in a user-visible retention footer
-- (`specs/EVL-02-experiments-and-side-by-side-diff.md:385`).
--
-- PARTITIONING CANNOT BE ALTERED AFTERWARDS -- changing it means rebuilding the
-- table. If item 9 needs a different key it must say so BEFORE this file is
-- applied.


-- ── datasets: the named entity ──────────────────────────────────────────────
--
-- ReplacingMergeTree(updated_at): rename and soft-delete are real, so a dataset
-- row is genuinely mutable and reads use FINAL. `updated_at` is NOT NULL, so the
-- VERSIONED form is legal here -- unlike `eval_runs`, which had to fall back to
-- the version-less form. Versioned is the better one: it keeps the row with the
-- highest `updated_at` rather than "whichever was written last", which is not
-- the same thing when two writers race.
--
-- WHY NO `item_count` COLUMN: it is a `count()` over `dataset_items FINAL WHERE
-- deleted = 0` at read time. A stored counter on a MergeTree needs a mutation
-- per write, which is the argument migration 17 already wrote down for
-- `hit_count` (`infra/dev/clickhouse/migrations/17_semantic_cache.sql:10-13`).
-- No number on this surface is a stored counter.
--
-- `name` is a plain String, NOT LowCardinality, and this deliberately diverges
-- from `prompts.name` in migration 03. LowCardinality builds a dictionary per
-- part -- worth it for a status vocabulary of four strings, a pure cost for free
-- text a user types once per dataset.
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS datasets (
    tenant_id    String,                     -- validated claim ONLY, never a body
    dataset_id   UUID,
    name         String,
    description  String DEFAULT '',
    -- Tombstone. A deleted dataset keeps its rows so that snapshots -- and the
    -- runs that cite them -- stay resolvable. Every read filters `deleted = 0`.
    deleted      UInt8 DEFAULT 0,
    created_at   DateTime64(3, 'UTC'),       -- MILLIS. datetime64_millis_now()
    created_by   String,                     -- claims sub
    updated_at   DateTime64(3, 'UTC')        -- MILLIS. the ReplacingMergeTree version
)
ENGINE = ReplacingMergeTree(updated_at)
ORDER BY (tenant_id, dataset_id)
PARTITION BY tuple();


-- ── dataset_items: the MUTABLE working set ──────────────────────────────────
--
-- ReplacingMergeTree(updated_at), and its mutability is the whole reason
-- `dataset_snapshot_items` exists. Two things move after creation: the `deleted`
-- tombstone, and the `expected_output` write-back that item 12's annotation loop
-- needs a landing zone for. Reads use FINAL.
--
-- `input` IS THE SAME SERIALIZATION THE EVAL ENGINE ALREADY DESERIALIZES:
-- `Vec<tracelane_shared::model::Message>`. Producer and consumer agree by
-- construction rather than by convention -- `CapturedInput` was built to match
-- (`crates/gateway/src/server.rs:3481-3492` says so at the site).
--
-- `system` IS SEPARATE FROM `input`, and GWY-45 learned this expensively: a
-- top-level `system` field is a DIFFERENT inbound shape from a
-- `role: "system"` message, and most of prod uses the former. Folding one into
-- the other loses which shape the customer actually sent.
--
-- `expected_output` IS Nullable AND WILL BE NULL ON EVERY TRACE-DERIVED ITEM.
-- Production captures INPUT ONLY. Measured on prod at the widest scope and
-- recorded in `specs/EVL-04-datasets-and-the-golden-schema.md:288-291` (2026-08-22,
-- not re-run here): 413 of 10,349 spans carry `gen_ai_input_messages` and 0 of
-- 10,349 carry `gen_ai_output_messages`, because the span is published BEFORE the
-- response-side guardrail seam so that a BLOCKED request still produces a span
-- (`crates/gateway/src/server.rs:3466-3479`). An empty string here would be a
-- silently wrong test case that passes nothing and fails nothing, so the column
-- is Nullable and NULL means "no reference", never "the reference is empty".
--
-- `input_hash` IS THE DEDUPE KEY, WITHIN ONE DATASET. sha256 hex over the
-- canonical `(messages, system)` serialization. `expected_output` is NOT in it:
-- two items with the same input are the same test case. NO SKIP INDEX -- the
-- ORDER BY prefix `(tenant_id, dataset_id)` already prunes to at most 200 rows
-- (the cap in `specs/EVL-04-...` section 5), and an index over 200 rows is
-- machinery that measures nothing.
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS dataset_items (
    tenant_id        String,                 -- validated claim ONLY, never a body
    dataset_id       UUID,
    item_id          UUID,
    -- Display/case label. `EvalCase.name` is NOT an Option
    -- (`EvalCase` in `crates/gateway/src/prompt_eval.rs`), so the resolver needs
    -- something to put there. Empty means unnamed -- the surface renders the
    -- ordinal, it does NOT invent a name.
    name             String DEFAULT '',
    input            String,                 -- JSON: Vec<shared::model::Message>
    system           String DEFAULT '',      -- the top-level `system` field
    expected_output  Nullable(String),       -- NULL = no reference. NEVER ''
    metadata         String DEFAULT '{}',    -- JSON object
    -- Provenance, set ONLY by the server-side copy path. An import that merely
    -- mentions a trace id does not get to claim one -- the "From traces" count
    -- means "we copied this from a span we read under this tenant's claim".
    source_trace_id  Nullable(UUID),
    source_span_id   String DEFAULT '',
    input_hash       FixedString(64),        -- sha256 hex, dedupe key
    deleted          UInt8 DEFAULT 0,        -- tombstone
    created_at       DateTime64(3, 'UTC'),   -- MILLIS. datetime64_millis_now()
    created_by       String,                 -- claims sub
    updated_at       DateTime64(3, 'UTC')    -- MILLIS. the version column
)
ENGINE = ReplacingMergeTree(updated_at)
ORDER BY (tenant_id, dataset_id, item_id)
PARTITION BY tuple();


-- ── dataset_snapshots: the frozen header ────────────────────────────────────
--
-- Plain MergeTree: a snapshot is written once and never amended. There is
-- nothing to replace, so no reader needs FINAL -- the argument migration 17
-- makes for `semantic_cache` at `:5-8`, including the observation that
-- `eval_runs` being ReplacingMergeTree has already cost this repo a reader that
-- must remember FINAL.
--
-- `snapshot_id` is a UUIDv5 over `(tenant_id, dataset_id, ordered item_hashes)`,
-- the same convention as `eval_suite_id_for`
-- (`eval_suite_id_for` in `crates/gateway/src/prompt_eval.rs`). So re-freezing an
-- unchanged dataset yields the SAME id and the writer writes nothing, and two runs that claim the
-- same inputs provably had them.
--
-- HONEST LIMIT, because MergeTree does not enforce it: idempotent re-freeze is a
-- WRITER property (check-then-write), not an engine one. Two concurrent freezes
-- of an unchanged dataset can both pass the check and write two byte-identical
-- rows. So a snapshot COUNT must be `uniqExact(snapshot_id)`, never `count()`.
-- The spec's section 3 `Snapshots` row says `count()` -- that is the spec being
-- loose, and the query is the thing that has to be right.
--
-- `item_count` IS stamped here even though nothing else on this surface stores a
-- counter, and the difference is that this one is a HISTORICAL FACT about an
-- immutable set rather than a running total. It cannot drift, because the thing
-- it counts cannot change.
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS dataset_snapshots (
    tenant_id    String,                     -- validated claim ONLY, never a body
    dataset_id   UUID,
    snapshot_id  UUID,                       -- UUIDv5 over the ordered item hashes
    item_count   UInt32,                     -- frozen with the snapshot
    created_at   DateTime64(3, 'UTC'),       -- MILLIS. datetime64_millis_now()
    created_by   String                      -- claims sub
)
ENGINE = MergeTree
ORDER BY (tenant_id, dataset_id, snapshot_id)
PARTITION BY tuple();


-- ── dataset_snapshot_items: THE POINT OF THE WHOLE DESIGN ───────────────────
--
-- A SNAPSHOT COPIES CONTENT. IT DOES NOT REFERENCE IT.
--
-- Two cheaper mechanisms were considered and both fail the same way:
--   * "items as of T" -- a time predicate over `dataset_items`. `dataset_items`
--     is a ReplacingMergeTree, so a LATER write for the same `item_id` replaces
--     the row the snapshot meant. The snapshot points at a moving target and
--     NOTHING ERRORS.
--   * "a manifest of item_ids" -- the same failure one level up. A manifest is a
--     set of references, and a referenced payload in a ReplacingMergeTree can
--     change under it.
--
-- So this table is a plain MergeTree holding a FULL COPY of every item's `input`,
-- `system`, `expected_output` and `metadata` at freeze time, with `ordinal`
-- fixing the order. This is the same argument that makes a dataset item copy
-- from a span rather than point at one (`spans` is a ReplacingMergeTree too --
-- `infra/dev/clickhouse/schema.sql:71`), applied one level up, and the
-- consistency is deliberate.
--
-- THE RESULT IS THE PROPERTY WE ACTUALLY WANT: the only table an experiment ever
-- reads is IMMUTABLE BY ENGINE. Nothing in the codebase can rewrite a MergeTree
-- row without a mutation, and no mutation is written against this table.
-- Reproducibility stops being a rule someone has to remember.
--
-- COST, bounded hard by the caps in the spec's section 5: at most 200 items x
-- 64 KiB is ~12.8 MiB per snapshot, at most 100 snapshots per dataset.
-- Duplication is the price of the guarantee and it is paid on purpose.
--
-- NO TTL, AND THAT IS THE POINT. `spans` expires at 365 days
-- (`infra/dev/clickhouse/schema.sql:80`). A snapshot that expired would silently
-- UN-REPRODUCE every run that cited it -- the run row would still be there,
-- still claiming a case set, with nothing left to check it against. A snapshot
-- outliving its source spans is exactly what makes a year-old run re-runnable.
-- Snapshots are capped by COUNT, never by age.
--
-- The retention consequence is a PRIVACY promise, not an implementation detail:
-- prompt text a customer expects to age out of `spans` persists indefinitely
-- inside a dataset they created. Deleting a dataset must therefore HARD-delete
-- its snapshot content -- see the spec's section 9 Q3, which is founder-owned.
--
-- ORDER BY omits `dataset_id` on purpose: `snapshot_id` is a UUIDv5 that already
-- has the dataset inside it, and the read is always
-- `WHERE tenant_id = ? AND snapshot_id = ?` in `ordinal` order. `dataset_id`
-- stays as a COLUMN so provenance survives even after the dataset is tombstoned.
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS dataset_snapshot_items (
    tenant_id        String,                 -- validated claim ONLY, never a body
    snapshot_id      UUID,
    ordinal          UInt32,                 -- 0-based. FIXES the case order
    dataset_id       UUID,                   -- provenance
    -- Provenance back to the live item. NOT a reference -- the live row may be
    -- tombstoned or its `expected_output` rewritten, and this copy does not care.
    item_id          UUID,
    name             String DEFAULT '',
    input            String,                 -- COPY. JSON: Vec<shared::model::Message>
    system           String DEFAULT '',      -- COPY
    expected_output  Nullable(String),       -- COPY. NULL = no reference, NEVER ''
    metadata         String DEFAULT '{}',    -- COPY
    input_hash       FixedString(64),        -- COPY. the snapshot_id is built from these
    source_trace_id  Nullable(UUID),
    source_span_id   String DEFAULT '',
    created_at       DateTime64(3, 'UTC')    -- MILLIS. freeze time, one per snapshot
)
ENGINE = MergeTree
ORDER BY (tenant_id, snapshot_id, ordinal)
PARTITION BY tuple();


-- ── eval_run_items: the durable per-case record ─────────────────────────────
--
-- Written by `execute_run` (`crates/gateway/src/prompt_eval.rs`), one row
-- per case, BEFORE the terminal `eval_runs` row -- the run row is the completion
-- marker, so the items must be durable first or a run can read `passed` with
-- missing items.
--
-- NOT AN OPTIMISATION. `results_json` is capped at 1 MiB
-- (`limits::RESULTS_JSON_BYTES`, `crates/gateway/src/prompt_eval.rs`) and 4 arms
-- x 200 items x an 8 KiB output is ~6.4 MiB, so the summary column physically cannot hold the detail.
-- Per-item rows are also what make item 9's aligned diff a JOIN instead of a
-- JSON parse.
--
-- PLAIN MergeTree, and this is a DELIBERATE DIVERGENCE from EVL-02's DDL block
-- (`specs/EVL-02-experiments-and-side-by-side-diff.md:143-161` writes
-- `ReplacingMergeTree()`). EVL-04's section 2.2 chose MergeTree with a reason:
-- an item row is TERMINAL ON WRITE -- a case result never changes -- so there is
-- nothing to replace and no reader needs FINAL. Migration 17 makes the identical
-- argument at `:5-8`.
-- THE COST OF THAT CHOICE, STATED SO IT IS NOT DISCOVERED LATER: MergeTree does
-- not collapse a re-inserted row, so the writer must insert a run's item block
-- EXACTLY ONCE. At most 200 rows is a single insert block and therefore atomic,
-- so this holds as long as nothing retries an insert it could not confirm. If
-- item 9 needs retry-idempotence the ENGINE has to change, and an engine change
-- is a table rebuild in a NEW migration -- never an edit to this file.
--
-- `cost_usd` IS Nullable AND NULL MEANS UNPRICED, NEVER ZERO. Summing unknown
-- costs as 0 is the exact coercion that made the spend tile under-report
-- silently, recorded at
-- `infra/dev/clickhouse/migrations/16_span_cost_attribution.sql:25-32`. Same for
-- `score`: NULL = no scorer produced a number, which is not `0.00`.
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS eval_run_items (
    tenant_id            String,             -- validated claim ONLY, never a body
    eval_run_id          UUID,               -- the `eval_runs` row this belongs to
    item_ordinal         UInt32,             -- position in the snapshot. STABLE
    -- All-zero UUID for an inline or trace-sourced case, as EVL-02 declares it
    -- (`:147`). A read path must test against the all-zero value explicitly and
    -- must not render it as an id.
    dataset_item_id      UUID,
    -- EVL-04 section 3 needs "Last run" per DATASET, and a dataset-sourced eval
    -- run can be started directly (`POST .../evals` with `source: dataset`) with
    -- no `experiments` row to join through. Without these two columns that number
    -- is uncomputable until item 9 ships. NULL = this run had no dataset behind
    -- it (an `Inline` or `Traces` source), which is a different fact from "the
    -- dataset is gone".
    dataset_id           Nullable(UUID),
    dataset_snapshot_id  Nullable(UUID),
    case_name            String,
    status               LowCardinality(String),  -- passed | failed | errored
    output               String,             -- <= CASE_OUTPUT_BYTES, marker if cut
    output_truncated     UInt8,              -- 1 = the output above is NOT complete
    scores               String DEFAULT '{}',     -- JSON {scorer_label: Float64}
    score                Nullable(Float64),  -- mean of `scores`. NULL = UNKNOWN
    latency_ms           UInt32,
    cost_usd             Nullable(Float64),  -- NULL = unpriced model. NEVER 0
    error                Nullable(String),   -- present iff status = 'errored'
    started_at           DateTime64(3, 'UTC')     -- MILLIS. datetime64_millis_now()
)
-- ENGINE SETTLED 2026-08-22 — `EVL-04` §2.2 said `MergeTree`, `EVL-02` said
-- `ReplacingMergeTree`, and the ENGINE is one of the three things an ALTER cannot
-- change, so it had to be decided BEFORE this file was applied anywhere.
--
-- ReplacingMergeTree WINS, and the deciding argument is not tidiness:
--   * `MergeTree` is only safe while nothing ever retries an insert it could not
--     CONFIRM. That is exactly the case a network blip produces, and this repo has
--     already chosen at-least-once as the safe direction everywhere it matters —
--     ingest acks AFTER the ClickHouse flush, and `spans`, `audit_log` and `prompts`
--     are all ReplacingMergeTree for this reason.
--   * A duplicated item row does not merely inflate a count. `pass_count /
--     (pass_count + fail_count)` is the number item 13's CI gate compares against a
--     `--threshold` to pass or FAIL somebody's merge. A double-counted item silently
--     moves a verdict, and it moves it in the flattering direction when the retried
--     case passed. A gate whose input can be double-counted is not a gate (§1).
--   * The ORDER BY below is already a natural dedup key — (tenant, run, ordinal)
--     identifies exactly one case in exactly one run — so replacing costs nothing
--     structurally.
-- `EVL-04` §2.2 is the losing spec and is corrected in the same change (§17).
ENGINE = ReplacingMergeTree
ORDER BY (tenant_id, eval_run_id, item_ordinal)
PARTITION BY toYYYYMM(started_at);


-- ── experiments: CREATED HERE, OWNED BY ITEM 9 ──────────────────────────────
--
-- EVL-04 CREATES THIS TABLE AND NEVER WRITES IT. It exists in migration 18 so
-- that item 9 (EVL-02) starts from an ALTER rather than from a second migration
-- that has to re-derive the dataset/snapshot linkage.
--
-- Columns are EVL-04 section 2.2's list. EVL-02's own DDL block
-- (`specs/EVL-02-experiments-and-side-by-side-diff.md:113-126`) spells three of
-- them differently and adds two more (`dataset_snapshot_id` for `snapshot_id`,
-- `created_by_user_id` for `created_by`, plus `item_count` and `notes`, and it
-- has no `updated_at` so it uses the version-less engine). EVL-04 is the spec
-- this file was built to, so its shape wins here. Item 9 reconciles in migration
-- `19_...`: `ADD COLUMN` for the two additions, `RENAME COLUMN` for the
-- spellings -- both are cheap on a table with no rows, and both are ALTERs.
-- WHAT AN ALTER CANNOT CHANGE is the ENGINE, the ORDER BY and the PARTITION BY.
-- Those three are the ones to argue about BEFORE this file is applied.
--
-- ReplacingMergeTree(updated_at) rather than the version-less form: an
-- experiment's `status` moves (running -> complete | errored), so the row is
-- mutable, and the versioned form keeps the row with the highest `updated_at`
-- instead of "whichever arrived last" -- which is not the same thing when a
-- status write races a rename. `updated_at` is NOT NULL, so it is legal as a
-- version column.
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS experiments (
    tenant_id      String,                   -- validated claim ONLY, never a body
    experiment_id  UUID,
    name           String,
    dataset_id     UUID,
    snapshot_id    UUID,                     -- the FROZEN set both arms read
    status         LowCardinality(String),   -- running | complete | errored
    created_at     DateTime64(3, 'UTC'),     -- MILLIS. datetime64_millis_now()
    created_by     String,                   -- claims sub
    updated_at     DateTime64(3, 'UTC')      -- MILLIS. the version column
)
ENGINE = ReplacingMergeTree(updated_at)
ORDER BY (tenant_id, experiment_id)
PARTITION BY tuple();
